<%# Shared explainer cards for the React Server Components demo (streaming and fallback views). %>
<section class="grid">
  <article class="card">
    <h2>What works here</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Async on the server.</strong>
        HelloServer can await data directly during render without pushing that fetch to the client.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Server stream first.</strong>
        The server-rendered content is the working demo. The LikeButton is the separate client-reference edge case.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Streaming output.</strong>
        stream_react_component streams HTML as the server component tree resolves.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card">
    <h2>What is intentionally separate</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Interactive client references.</strong>
        The LikeButton path is visibly marked as limited while the upstream streaming hydration support settles.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Public RSC composition.</strong>
        Use <a href="/rsc-showcase">/rsc-showcase</a> for the RSC payload route that composes server content beside ordinary client React.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card">
    <h2>Inspect these files next</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <code>app/javascript/src/HelloServer/</code><br>
        Source for the generated server component example and client island.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>app/controllers/hello_server_controller.rb</code><br>
        Rails controller that sets props and streams the RSC view.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>app/views/hello_server/index.html.erb</code><br>
        Rails view that calls stream_react_component.
      </li>
      <li>
        <code>client/node-renderer.js</code><br>
        Node renderer entrypoint used by the Pro SSR and RSC stack.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </article>

  <article class="card">
    <h2>Learn more</h2>
